1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:01,900
Reading

2
00:00:02,780 --> 00:00:05,660
The Million Pound Bank Note

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:09,800
Act 1 Scene 3

4
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:12,480
It is the summer of 1903.

5
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:16,140
Two old and wealthy brothers, Roderick and Oliver,

6
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:17,240
have made a bet.

7
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:23,840
Oliver believes that with a million pound bank note a man could survive a month in London.

8
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:26,940
His brother Roderick doubts it.

9
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:33,020
At this moment they see a penniless young man wandering on the pavement outside their house.

10
00:00:33,780 --> 00:00:36,480
It is Henry Adams, an American businessman,

11
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:40,880
who is lost in London and does not know what he should do.

12
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:44,040
Young man, would you step inside a moment,

13
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:44,600
please?

14
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:46,540
Who, me, sir?

15
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:48,820
Yes, you.

16
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:51,520
Through the front door on your left.

17
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:55,600
Ahhhhhhhhhh.

18
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:56,240
Thanks.

19
00:00:57,380 --> 00:01:00,120
Good morning, sir. Would you please come in?

20
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:02,320
Permit me to lead the way, sir.

21
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:05,580
Thank you, James. That will be all.

22
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:09,540
How do you do? Mr. Errr...

23
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:11,140
Adams. Henry Adams.

24
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:13,660
Come and sit down, Mr. Adams.

25
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:14,860
Thank you.

26
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:16,200
You're an American?

27
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:18,940
That's right. From San Francisco.

28
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:21,220
How well do you know London?

29
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:23,980
Not at all. It's my first trip here.

30
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:28,200
I wonder, Mr. Adams, if you'd mind us asking a few questions?

31
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:30,560
Not at all. Go right ahead.

32
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:35,020
May we ask what you're doing in this country and what your plans are?

33
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:38,820
Well, I can't say that I have any plans.

34
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:42,540
I'm hoping to find work. As a matter of fact,

35
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:44,660
I landed in Britain by accident.

36
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:46,520
How is that possible?

37
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:50,960
Well, you see, back home I had my own boat.

38
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:55,580
About a month ago I was sailing out of the bay.

39
00:01:56,640 --> 00:01:58,040
Well, go on.

40
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:05,340
Oh, yes. Well, towards nightfall I found myself carried out to sea by a strong wind.

41
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:09,600
It was all my fault. I didn't know whether I could survive

42
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:10,520
until morning.

43
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:16,460
The next morning I'd just about given myself up for lost when I was spotted by a ship.

44
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:19,380
And it was the ship that brought you to England?

45
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:25,200
Yes. The fact is that I earned my passage by working as an unpaid hand,

46
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:27,120
which accounts for my appearance.

47
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:30,820
I went to the American Embassy to seek help,

48
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:32,720
but...

49
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:36,300
Well, you mustn't worry about that. It's an advantage.

50
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:39,520
I'm afraid I don't quite follow you,

51
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:39,780
sir.

52
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:44,140
Tell us, Mr. Adams, what sort of work did you do in America?

53
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:49,760
I worked for a mining company. Could you offer me some kind of work here?

54
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:53,500
Patience, Mr. Adams. If you don't mind,

55
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:56,620
may I ask you how much money you have?

56
00:02:57,720 --> 00:03:00,360
Well, to be honest, I have none.

57
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:03,920
What luck! Brother, what luck!

58
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:06,620
Well, it may seem lucky to you,

59
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:07,580
but not to me.

60
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:11,280
On the contrary, in fact, if this is your idea of some kind of joke,

61
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:12,640
I don't think it's very funny.

62
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:15,700
Now, if you'll excuse me, I think I'll be on my way.

63
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:21,560
Please don't go, Mr. Adams. You mustn't think we don't care about you.

64
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:24,840
Oliver, give him the letter. Yes,

65
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:27,440
the letter. The letter.

66
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:28,500
For me?

67
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:29,780
For you.

68
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:36,560
Oh, no, you mustn't open it. Not yet. You can't open it until two o'clock.

69
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:38,380
Oh, this is silly.

70
00:03:38,980 --> 00:03:41,780
Not silly. There's money in it.

71
00:03:42,260 --> 00:03:42,740
James!

72
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:47,380
Oh, no, I don't want your charity. I just want an honest job.

73
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,620
We know you're hardworking. That's why we've given you the letter.

74
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:54,440
James, show Mr. Adams out.

75
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:56,660
Good luck, Mr. Adams.

76
00:03:56,660 --> 00:03:59,900
Well, why don't you explain what this is all about?

77
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:04,520
You'll soon know, in exactly an hour and a half.

78
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:10,140
This way, sir. Mr. Adams, not until two o'clock. Promise?

79
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:12,540
Promise. Goodbye.
